# LF in the repository, on every machine.
#
# Carried from the monorepo this split out of, where its absence cost a real
# failure: a fresh clone on the Windows machine that wrote the code failed a test
# that same machine's working tree passed, because `core.autocrlf` converted LF
# to CRLF on checkout and a regex containing `,\n` cannot match `,\r\n`.
#
# It matters more here than it did there. `go test` reads golden strings, and
# `count_test.go` asserts on a reply cut at a 4096-byte frame boundary - a test
# whose entire subject is exact bytes. A line ending is a byte.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, explicitly. `text=auto` gets this right by sniffing and a misdetected
# binary is a corrupted one - the failure here that does not announce itself.
# CRLF FOR BATCH FILES, against the repository-wide LF above. cmd.exe is the one
# consumer here that is not Go: a `.bat` with LF endings works for simple lines
# and misbehaves on the multi-line constructs both files use, and it does it at
# RUN time on an owner's machine rather than in CI. These two files exist precisely so a non-technical
# owner never opens a shell, so they are the last place to accept that risk.
*.bat text eol=crlf

*.exe binary
*.png binary
